 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "ROBIN HOOD. A bronze outlaw with a bow, striding out by the castle gates since 1952. His arrow has been pinched so many times that nobody has kept count. He is smiling anyway."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "NOTTINGHAM CASTLE. It sits on a sandstone rock honeycombed with caves. The old castle was pulled down after the Civil War; the grand house that replaced it was burned by rioters in 1831. It is much calmer now."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE CAVES. Beneath Nottingham are more than eight hundred hand-cut caves, used for cellars, tanneries, beer and wartime shelters. The pub next door claims it was founded in 1189. The caves keep their own counsel."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE LIONS. Two stone lions guard the Council House, and Nottingham folk say they will 'meet you by the lions' with a certainty that has seen off many a mobile phone. They're called Leo and Oscar. Neither has ever been late."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE OLD MARKET SQUARE. One of the biggest city squares in Britain, and home to the Goose Fair for centuries until it moved out in 1928. The Fair is over seven hundred years old and still sells apples the size of your head."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE LACE MARKET. In Victorian times Nottingham made most of the world's machine-made lace, and its merchants built tall red-brick warehouses to show it off. The lace has gone. Now they sell cake."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "THE TRENT. The big river of the Midlands, wide and brown and slow. On its banks stand two football grounds and a cricket ground, all rather proud of their trophies. The reeds are full of birds."
  }
 ],
